confusing. Frank was thinking of replicating most across the facts

Conventions for all seven queries:

_bk columns are the source GUIDs. The load swaps them for surrogate keys through the dim lookups.
_code columns are choice values that arrive as numbers like 100000000. The labels live in the option-set metadata, so check whether your lake has it.
Correction: asqa_origin is a choice, not a lookup, so dim_origin has to be built from those labels.
Anything commented out is parked until the BA confirms it.

1. fact_submission (header, one row per submission)

sql
-- TABLE: fact_submission | header fact, the hub every other fact joins to
-- SOURCE: dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission
SELECT
    s.Id                              AS submission_bk,
    s.asqa_regsubid                   AS regsubid,
    s.asqa_requestreferenceid         AS request_reference_id,
    s.asqa_registrationsubmissiontype AS type_bk,         -- dim_submission_type
    s.asqa_account                    AS rto_bk,          -- dim_rto
    s.asqa_submittedby                AS submitted_by_bk, -- dim_contact
    s.asqa_submissionmethod           AS method_bk,       -- dim_submission_method
    s.asqa_origin                     AS origin_code,     -- choice
    s.statecode,
    s.statuscode,
    s.asqa_submittedDate              AS submitted_date,
    s.asqa_approved                   AS is_approved,
    s.createdon,
    s.modifiedon
    -- parked: asqa_potentiallapse, asqa_evidenceofdelivery,
    --         asqa_publishedtothenationalregister, asqa_readytopublish, asqa_statusupdate
FROM dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s
WHERE s.IsDelete IS NULL;
Captures: the event “an RTO lodged a request”.
Measures: the count only, by type, family, RTO, month and status.
Why: this answers most reporting on its own. Turnaround needs a decision or completion date, and those are empty in dev. Confirm in prod before promising turnaround.

2. fact_renewal (one row per renewal record)

sql
-- TABLE: fact_renewal | renewal applications, including ones no submission points at
-- SOURCE: dbo.asqa_rtorenewal, joined back to the submission
SELECT
    r.Id                                AS renewal_bk,
    s.Id                                AS submission_bk,   -- NULL becomes -1 in the load
    r.asqa_account                      AS rto_bk,
    r.asqa_renewaltype                  AS renewal_type_code,
    r.asqa_typeofregistrationforrenew   AS registration_type_code,
    r.asqa_typeofregistrationforrenewal AS registration_type_code_2, -- near-duplicate column, confirm
    r.asqa_renewalsubmitted             AS renewal_submitted_code,
    r.asqa_setrenewalduedate            AS set_renewal_due_date,
    r.statecode,
    r.statuscode,
    r.createdon,
    r.modifiedon
FROM dbo.asqa_rtorenewal r
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s
       ON s.asqa_rtorenewal = r.Id AND s.IsDelete IS NULL
WHERE r.IsDelete IS NULL;
Captures: a renewal application.
Measures: the count, plus due date against created date for timeliness.
Why: 28 renewals have no submission, so this can’t live inside the header. The ~55 declaration answers and the reopen contact details stay out. One is form content and the other is personal information.

3. fact_registration_end (withdrawal or closure)

sql
-- TABLE: fact_registration_end | requests that end a registration
-- SOURCE: dbo.asqa_rtowithdrawal UNION ALL dbo.asqa_rtoclosure
SELECT
    w.Id AS registration_end_bk, 'Withdrawal' AS end_type, s.Id AS submission_bk,
    w.asqa_account AS rto_bk,
    w.asqa_preferredwithdrawaleffectivedate AS effective_date,  -- RTO's preferred date
    w.statecode, w.statuscode, w.createdon, w.modifiedon
FROM dbo.asqa_rtowithdrawal w
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s
       ON s.asqa_rtowithdrawal = w.Id AND s.IsDelete IS NULL
WHERE w.IsDelete IS NULL
UNION ALL
SELECT
    c.Id, 'Closure', s.Id,
    c.asqa_account_rtoclosure,
    c.asqa_dateofeffect,
    c.statecode, c.statuscode, c.createdon, c.modifiedon
FROM dbo.asqa_rtoclosure c
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s
       ON s.asqa_rtoclosure = c.Id AND s.IsDelete IS NULL
WHERE c.IsDelete IS NULL;
Captures: a request to end a registration.
Measures: the count and the effective date, so time from submission to effective.
Why: “when do registrations actually end” needs a date the header doesn’t have. Withdrawal’s date is the RTO’s preferred date, not a confirmed one. Closure’s four VET due and received date pairs stay out until someone asks.

4. fact_measurement_period (lapse or extension)

sql
-- TABLE: fact_measurement_period | lapse and extension records about delivery periods
-- SOURCE: dbo.asqa_rtolapsing UNION ALL dbo.asqa_rtoextendmeasurementperiod
SELECT
    l.Id AS measurement_period_bk, 'rtolapsing' AS source_entity, s.Id AS submission_bk,
    l.asqa_rtoaccount AS rto_bk,
    l.asqa_measurementperiodstartdate AS measurement_period_start,
    l.asqa_measurementperiodenddate   AS measurement_period_end,
    l.asqa_nildeliverystartdate       AS nil_delivery_start,
    l.asqa_nildeliveryenddate         AS nil_delivery_end,
    l.asqa_extensionstartdate         AS extension_start,
    l.asqa_extensionenddate           AS extension_end,
    l.asqa_reqedextensionstartdate    AS requested_extension_start,
    l.asqa_requestedextensionenddate  AS requested_extension_end,
    l.asqa_lastdateofdelivery         AS last_date_of_delivery,
    l.statecode, l.statuscode, l.createdon, l.modifiedon
FROM dbo.asqa_rtolapsing l
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s
       ON s.asqa_rtolapsing = l.Id AND s.IsDelete IS NULL
WHERE l.IsDelete IS NULL
UNION ALL
SELECT
    e.Id, 'rtoextendmeasurementperiod', s.Id,
    e.asqa_account,
    NULL, NULL,
    e.asqa_nildeliverydatefrom, e.asqa_nildeliverydateto,
    e.asqa_periodextensiondatefrom, e.asqa_periodextensiondateto, -- assumed same meaning, confirm
    NULL, NULL, NULL,
    e.statecode, e.statuscode, e.createdon, e.modifiedon
FROM dbo.asqa_rtoextendmeasurementperiod e
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s
       ON s.asqa_rtoextendmeasurementperiod = e.Id AND s.IsDelete IS NULL
WHERE e.IsDelete IS NULL;
Captures: lapse and extension records tied to an RTO’s delivery measurement period.
Measures: counts and durations in days (period, nil-delivery, extension), calculated from the dates.
Why: these dates exist nowhere else. Treating the two extension date pairs as equivalent is my assumption.

5. fact_ownership_change (one row per change)

sql
-- TABLE: fact_ownership_change | a change of ownership of an RTO
-- SOURCE: dbo.asqa_rtoownership
SELECT
    o.Id                           AS ownership_change_bk,
    s.Id                           AS submission_bk,
    o.asqa_accountid               AS rto_bk,
    o.asqa_dateofeffect            AS date_of_effect,
    o.asqa_totalpercentageofshares AS total_percentage_of_shares,
    TRY_CAST(o.asqa_noofenrolledstudents AS int)     AS students_enrolled,      -- text in source
    TRY_CAST(o.asqa_noofstudentpriortochange AS int) AS students_before_change, -- text in source
    o.asqa_has50percentagesharechanged AS shares_over_50_changed_code,
    o.asqa_ownershipstatus             AS ownership_status_code,
    o.asqa_dateoflastdeliveryoftraining AS date_of_last_delivery,
    o.statecode, o.statuscode, o.createdon, o.modifiedon
FROM dbo.asqa_rtoownership o
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s
       ON s.asqa_rtoownership = o.Id AND s.IsDelete IS NULL
WHERE o.IsDelete IS NULL;
Captures: an ownership change.
Measures: the count, total share % and the number of students affected.
Why: this carries the effective date and student impact. The accountant contact details stay out because they’re personal.

6. fact_ownership_share (one row per owner per change)

sql
-- TABLE: fact_ownership_share | each owner's stake within a change
-- SOURCE: dbo.asqa_rtoowner
SELECT
    w.Id                         AS ownership_share_bk,
    s.Id                         AS submission_bk,       -- resolved here so reports skip the change table
    w.asqa_rtoownership          AS ownership_change_bk, -- plain column, groups shares by change
    w.asqa_rtoaccount            AS rto_bk,
    w.asqa_person                AS owner_person_bk,
    w.asqa_companyid             AS owner_company_bk,
    w.asqa_ownertype             AS owner_type_code,
    w.asqa_dateofeffect          AS date_of_effect,
    w.asqa_percentageofshares    AS percentage_of_shares,
    w.asqa_newpercentageofshares AS new_percentage_of_shares,
    w.statecode, w.statuscode, w.createdon, w.modifiedon
FROM dbo.asqa_rtoowner w
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s
       ON s.asqa_rtoownership = w.asqa_rtoownership AND s.IsDelete IS NULL
WHERE w.IsDelete IS NULL;
Captures: each owner’s stake, before and after.
Measures: share % and new share %. They add up within one change, never across changes.
Why: there are many owners per change, so it can’t sit in the change row without fan-out. Owner names stay out because they’re personal.

7. fact_scope_item (one row per training product)

sql
-- TABLE: fact_scope_item | each qualification, course, unit or skill set in a scope request
-- SOURCE: dbo.asqa_rtoscopeitem, via dbo.asqa_rtoscope and the submission
SELECT
    i.Id                          AS scope_item_bk,
    COALESCE(s1.Id, s2.Id, sc.asqa_registrationsubmission) AS submission_bk, -- -1 if all NULL
    i.asqa_rtoscope               AS scope_bk,
    i.asqa_account                AS rto_bk,
    i.asqa_qualification          AS qualification_bk,
    i.asqa_course                 AS course_bk,
    i.asqa_unit                   AS unit_bk,
    i.asqa_skillset               AS skillset_bk,
    i.asqa_scopeitemtype          AS scope_item_type_code,
    i.asqa_scopeitemstatus        AS scope_item_status_code,
    i.asqa_decision               AS decision_code,
    i.asqa_isrefused              AS is_refused,
    i.asqa_typeofregistration     AS registration_type_code,
    i.asqa_estimatedtotaltuitionfees    AS estimated_tuition_fees,
    i.asqa_estimatedtotalnontuitionfees AS estimated_non_tuition_fees,
    i.asqa_totalestimatedcost           AS total_estimated_cost,
    i.asqa_numberofdomesticstudents       AS domestic_students,       -- a second "totalnumberof" pair exists, confirm
    i.asqa_numberofinternationalstudents  AS international_students,
    i.asqa_totalduration                AS total_duration,
    i.asqa_tuitioninweeks               AS tuition_weeks,
    i.asqa_holidaybreaksinweeks         AS holiday_break_weeks,
    i.asqa_hoursforworkbasedtraining    AS work_based_training_hours,
    i.asqa_weeksforworkbasedtraining    AS work_based_training_weeks,
    i.statecode, i.statuscode, i.createdon, i.modifiedon
FROM dbo.asqa_rtoscopeitem i
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_rtoscope sc
       ON sc.Id = i.asqa_rtoscope AND sc.IsDelete IS NULL
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s1
       ON s1.asqa_rtoscopeapplication = sc.Id AND s1.IsDelete IS NULL
LEFT JOIN dbo.asqa_registrationsubmission s2
       ON s2.asqa_rtoscopeitem = i.Id AND s2.IsDelete IS NULL
WHERE i.IsDelete IS NULL;
Captures: each training product an RTO asks to add or amend.
Measures: fees, total cost, student numbers, durations and hours. These are the only real numeric measures, and they add up across items.
Why: there are many items per submission, and at least 45 scope rows aren’t linked to one. The currency _base columns and exchange rate are left out as duplicates. Before loading, check the row count isn’t inflated by two submissions pointing at the same scope.

Should the other source columns be copied into every fact? No.

Copying 195 columns into 8 facts puts the same unexplained columns in 8 places, with the nulls multiplied by 8. When copies drift apart, nobody knows which one is right. Nulls plus no data dictionary are a reason to hold a column back, not to copy it.
Nothing is lost. Every column stays in cleansed, and your pipeline sheet is metadata-driven, so adding a column to a fact later is cheap.
Frank is right about one part. The shared columns do belong on every fact. That’s the repeated set in the queries above: the RTO key, the submission key, state and status, and created and modified dates.
His “what if someone needs column X” worry has three answers. Common columns are reachable by joining through submission_sk to the header. Narrative and declaration columns go into a keyed detail table if someone asks. Anything else gets added to the fact on request.

A replacement for the missing data dictionary: a column register with one row per source column, an include/exclude flag and a reason code (platform, label copy, audit, PII, form answer, parked for BA). That’s the defensible answer to “why isn’t column X in gold”. I can generate the first draft from the schema exports you’ve sent. Want that next?
